INT. 24-HOUR LAUNDROMAT, SUNSET STRIP PLAZA - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent hum. Rows of dryers tumbling, each a small bright porthole. Plastic chairs bolted to the floor. A vending machine glows red beside a bulletin board layered with years of flyers.

GWENDOLYN "GWEN" ABERNATHY-TSUJI (50s, orthopedic shoes, a nurse's lanyard still around her neck) sits alone in the middle of the row, folding towels. She has folded the same towel three times. Her cell phone lies face-up on the seat beside her, silent.

She stacks the towel on a pile. Reaches for another. The phone buzzes. She does not look at it for four full buzzes. Then she picks it up.

GWEN
Hello?

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mom. It's me.

GWEN
I know who it is, baby.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Did I wake you?

GWEN
I don't sleep, Kobe. You know that.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Still on nights?

GWEN
Still on nights.

A dryer buzzes. She stands, opens it, pulls out a handful of warm laundry, and holds it to her face, just for a moment. Then she carries it to the folding table.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
How's the knee?

GWEN
Fine.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mom.

GWEN
It's a knee, Kobe. It's seen worse.

She snaps a pillowcase flat. Folds it in thirds.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm not calling about the knee.

GWEN
I know.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
You do?

GWEN
You called at three in the morning. You're a good boy. You don't do that for nothing.

Silence on the line. She waits. Outside, a bus idles at the stop, its brake lights painting the window red.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mom. I got the letter.

Her hands stop.

GWEN
Which letter?

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
The one from Dad's lawyer.

Gwen sets down the pillowcase. She walks to the window and stands with her back to the room, one hand pressed flat to the cold glass.

GWEN
What did it say?

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
That he left something. For both of us.

GWEN
He left us nothing.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mom, I'm looking at a deed.

GWEN
I don't want it.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
It's the house in Accra. The one he said he sold.

GWEN
He sold it. I watched him sign.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mom --

GWEN
He sold it, Kwabena!

Her voice cracks and fills the whole room. The dryers tumble on, indifferent. A long breath over the line.

GWEN (CONT'D)
(quietly)
I'm sorry. I didn't mean to shout.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
It's okay.

GWEN
It isn't. I just -- I carried that man for thirty years.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
I know.

GWEN
(together)
I'm not carrying him anymore.

KWABENA (V.O.) ^
(together, over phone)
You don't have to carry him anymore.

They both go still. The line holds open between them, hissing softly.

GWEN
Say that again.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
You don't have to, Mom. I'll go. I'll sort it out. You can stay right where you are.

She sinks into a plastic chair. The bus pulls away from the stop, and its light slides off her face.

GWEN
When are you flying?

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Thursday.

GWEN
I'll make the jollof.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Mom, I'm not staying for dinner.

GWEN
You are staying for dinner.

KWABENA (V.O.)
(over phone)
Yes, Mom.

She laughs, a real laugh, wet at the edges. She presses the heel of her hand to her eye. Behind her, the last dryer buzzes and falls silent. In the sudden quiet, she is very small, and very still, and finally not alone.
